WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.300
Owning a chart isn’t enough.

2
00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.750
Keeping it corrected is a basic duty, and here’s the routine.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.350
Start with the chart’s edition date.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.675
Check every Local Notice to Mariners issued since that date for corrections that touch your chart.

5
00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:22.375
On paper, mark each correction neatly, and record it in a log, so you can prove the chart is current.

6
00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:26.550
On a chartplotter, install the chart updates regularly.

7
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.175
Here’s a question to ask yourself.

8
00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:32.650
How old is the chart card in your plotter right now?

9
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.650
If you don’t know, it’s probably years old.

10
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.975
And when two sources disagree, use the most recent one, and go carefully.

11
00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:44.125
That’s lesson six.

12
00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:46.950
The Coast Pilot says what the chart can’t.

13
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.525
The Local Notice is weekly.

14
00:00:49.775 --> 00:00:53.150
Thirty-three for the water, forty-six for the boat.

15
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.550
Next time: tides.
